2=\"419.5\" y2=\"259\" stroke=\"#3f2c23\"/><text x=\"419.5\" y=\"273\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"svgc\">10</text><line x1=\"540.0\" y1=\"254\" x2=\"540.0\" y2=\"259\" stroke=\"#3f2c23\"/><text x=\"540.0\" y=\"273\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"svgc\">100</text><line x1=\"53\" y1=\"254.0\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"254.0\" stroke=\"#3f2c23\"/><text x=\"49\" y=\"258.0\" text-anchor=\"end\" class=\"svgc\">0</text><line x1=\"53\" y1=\"136.0\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"136.0\" stroke=\"#3f2c23\"/><text x=\"49\" y=\"140.0\" text-anchor=\"end\" class=\"svgc\">0.5</text><line x1=\"53\" y1=\"18.0\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"18.0\" stroke=\"#3f2c23\"/><text x=\"49\" y=\"22.0\" text-anchor=\"end\" class=\"svgc\">1</text><text x=\"299.0\" y=\"292\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"svgd\">brake over best brake, κ/κ*</text><text x=\"16\" y=\"136.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"svgd\" transform=\"rotate(-90 16 136.0)\">P / P*</text></svg><figcaption>Figure 3. The harvested power (2.3) over its maximum (2.5), against the brake over the best brake (2.4): $\\bar P/P^* = 2x/(1+x^2)$ with $x = \\kappa/\\kappa^*$, the same curve at every wind and for every second blade. The top is flat: a brake 0.27 times the best, or 3.7 times it, still gives half the power ($x = 2 \\mp \\sqrt3$).</figcaption></figure>",
  "chaos_text": "<p>A pendulum driven periodically and braked can move chaotically: two copies started a hair apart drift apart exponentially, like two leaves dropped side by side into a stream. Our second blade is such a pendulum, (1.5), so the question must be asked. The rate of that drift is the <b>largest Lyapunov exponent</b> $\\Lambda$: start two copies $10^{-8}$ apart, follow them, rescale their separation back every quarter turn of the rotor, and divide the logarithm of the total growth by the number of turns. A regular, damped motion gives $\\Lambda < 0$; chaos gives $\\Lambda > 0$. Because chaos depends on where the motion starts, every case is run from 8 different starts, the second blade at rest on the mother blade's line and 7 at random angles and rates, some tumbling right over at first; and at the best brake and at a tenth and a hundredth of it, where a lightly braked swing has the most freedom.</p><div class=\"press\"><div class=\"ctag\">On our blade</div><p class=\"tcap\">Table 2. The largest Lyapunov exponent per turn, the largest over the starts, at each wind and brake; beside it the decay rate per turn of a small disturbance of the braked spring, $2\\pi\\,\\max\\,\\mathrm{Re}\\,s$ with $s^2 + \\kappa s + R = 0$.</p><table class=\"mt\"><thead><tr><th>wind</th><th>$G$</th><th>brake</th><th>$\\Lambda$ per turn</th><th>small-swing decay</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>6 m/s</td><td>28.45</td><td>1 $\\kappa^*$</td><td>−6.50</td><td>−6.42</td></tr>\n<tr><td>6 m/s</td><td>28.45</td><td>0.1 $\\kappa^*$</td><td>−29.36</td><td>−29.37</td></tr>\n<tr><td>6 m/s</td><td>28.45</td><td>0.01 $\\kappa^*$</td><td>−2.92</td><td>−2.94</td></tr>\n<tr><td>8 m/s</td><td>16.00</td><td>1 $\\kappa^*$</td><td>−6.44</td><td>−6.42</td></tr>\n<tr><td>8 m/s</td><td>16.00</td><td>0.1 $\\kappa^*$</td><td>−29.36</td><td>−29.37</td></tr>\n<tr><td>8 m/s</td><td>16.00</td><td>0.01 $\\kappa^*$</td><td>−2.92</td><td>−2.94</td></tr>\n<tr><td>10 m/s</td><td>10.24</td><td>1 $\\kappa^*$</td><td>−6.43</td><td>−6.42</td></tr>\n<tr><td>10 m/s</td><td>10.24</td><td>0.1 $\\kappa^*$</td><td>−29.36</td><td>−29.37</td></tr>\n<tr><td>10 m/s</td><td>10.24</td><td>0.01 $\\kappa^*$</td><td>−2.92</td><td>−2.94</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\nEvery exponent is negative: every start, at every wind and brake, ends on the same regular, periodic swing. More than that, each exponent is the decay rate of a small disturbance of the braked centrifugal spring alone, to within 1.2 %: the motion forgets its start as fast as a linear, damped spring forgets a nudge. Halving the step changes the exponents by at most 0.7 % of their size. The physical reason is the centrifugal spring: at $R = 94.5$ it is 3.3 to 9.2 times stronger than gravity's push, so whatever the start, the second blade is pulled back to the mother blade's line and swings there in step with the push; chaos needs a spring and a push of similar strength, near resonance, and section 2.5 showed that a second blade never gets there.</div>",
  "generator": "<p class=\"tcap\">Table 3. The maxon RE 25 with a GP 32 C gearhead in the hinge, at the ratio (492 : 1 at every wind) and load the formula (7.6)–(7.7) finds best. Swing and load are given as formula / full: the describing-function formula, and the full equation (7.8). Paid: what the rotor pays; the last two columns are the shares of it that reach the load and that friction takes.</p><table class=\"mt\"><thead><tr><th>wind</th><th>$R_L$, Ω</th><th>swing, rad</th><th>load, mW</th><th>paid, mW</th><th>load</th><th>friction</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>6 m/s</td><td>9.24</td><td>0.220 / 0.221</td><td>104.2 / 106.1</td><td>271.3</td><td>39 %</td><td>14 %</td></tr>\n<tr><td>8 m/s</td><td>7.03</td><td>0.127 / 0.124</td><td>74.0 / 75.5</td><td>203.3</td><td>37 %</td><td>15 %</td></tr>\n<tr><td>10 m/s</td><td>5.73</td><td>0.083 / 0.080</td><td>56.1 / 57.2</td><td>161.9</td><td>35 %</td><td>15 %</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>",
  "sizing": "<p class=\"tcap\">Table 4. The share of the best power $P^*$ the RE 25 + GP 32 C delivers to its load, against the mass of a 1 m second blade; mean over the three winds, and the ratio and load power at 8 m/s.</p><table class=\"mt\"><thead><tr><th>mass (kg)</th><th>share of $P^*$</th><th>ratio at 8 m/s</th><th>load at 8 m/s (mW)</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>0.01</td><td>7.1 %</td><td>14</td><td>0.14</td></tr>\n<tr><td>0.02</td><td>13.6 %</td><td>33</td><td>0.54</td></tr>\n<tr><td>0.05</td><td>16.8 %</td><td>51</td><td>1.66</td></tr>\n<tr><td>0.1</td><td>25.3 %</td><td>111</td><td>5.07</td></tr>\n<tr><td>0.2</td><td>31.5 %</td><td>190</td><td>12.65</td></tr>\n<tr><td>0.5</td><td>30.6 %</td><td>492</td><td>29.96</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>36.8 %</td><td>492</td><td>73.96</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2</td><td>33.3 %</td><td>492</td><td>133.33</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5</td><td>5.9 %</td><td>246</td><td>56.84</td></tr>\n<tr><td>10</td><td>3.0 %</td><td>246</td><td>57.34</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>",
  "sizes": "<p class=\"tcap\">Table 5. The second blade over the range of real tip devices: length $l$, mass per length, mass $m$, first moment $ml/2$; the best power $P^*$ (2.5) at 6 and 10 m/s; the generator torque $\\tau^* = \\tfrac{ml}{2}g/\\sqrt2$ it must hold; the extra pull on the mother blade's tip at 10 m/s, $m\\Omega^2(r_p + l/2)$; and what the RE 25 + GP 32 C delivers to its load at 6 m/s. The row in bold is the size these notes use.</p><table class=\"mt\"><thead><tr><th>$l$, m</th><th>kg/m</th><th>$m$, kg</th><th>$ml/2$, kg m</th><th>$P^*$ 6 m/s, W</th><th>$P^*$ 10 m/s, W</th><th>$\\tau^*$, N m</th><th>pull, kN</th><th>RE 25, mW</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>0.5</td><td>1</td><td>0.5</td><td>0.125</td><td>0.0667</td><td>0.0400</td><td>0.867</td><td>0.0454</td><td>23.5</td></tr>\n<tr><td>0.5</td><td>3</td><td>1.5</td><td>0.375</td><td>0.200</td><td>0.120</td><td>2.60</td><td>0.136</td><td>73.5</td></tr>\n<tr><td>0.5</td><td>10</td><td>5</td><td>1.25</td><td>0.667</td><td>0.400</td><td>8.67</td><td>0.454</td><td>93.9</td></tr>\n<tr><td>0.5</td><td>30</td><td>15</td><td>3.75</td><td>2.00</td><td>1.20</td><td>26.0</td><td>1.36</td><td>100</td></tr>\n<tr style=\"font-weight:600\"><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0.5</td><td>0.268</td><td>0.161</td><td>3.47</td><td>0.0912</td><td>104</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>3</td><td>3</td><td>1.5</td><td>0.805</td><td>0.483</td><td>10.4</td><td>0.274</td><td>96.9</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>10</td><td>10</td><td>5</td><td>2.68</td><td>1.61</td><td>34.7</td><td>0.912</td><td>102</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>30</td><td>30</td><td>15</td><td>8.05</td><td>4.83</td><td>104</td><td>2.74</td><td>102</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>1.08</td><td>0.651</td><td>13.9</td><td>0.184</td><td>101</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2</td><td>3</td><td>6</td><td>6</td><td>3.25</td><td>1.95</td><td>41.6</td><td>0.551</td><td>104</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2</td><td>10</td><td>20</td><td>20</td><td>10.8</td><td>6.51</td><td>139</td